@page "/"

<PageTitle>Blazor-ApexMaps</PageTitle>

<h1>Blazor-ApexMaps</h1>

<p class="lead">
    Interactive geographic data visualization for Blazor: choropleths, proportional symbols,
    markers with clustering, great-circle arcs and routes, on 13 projections and 26 built-in
    geometry packs.
</p>

<div class="demo-card">
    <ApexMaps Options="_options" Height="440" />
</div>

<p class="text-muted small">
    Two option keys and no styling. The scale, the classification, the legend, the tooltip, the
    projection and the framing are all defaults.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">That map, in full</h2>

<pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;ApexMaps Options="_options" Height="440" /&gt;

@@code {
    private readonly MapsOptions _options = new()
    {
        Geo = new GeoOptions { Map = GeoMaps.WorldCountries110m },
        Series =
        [
            new ChoroplethSeries
            {
                Name = "Renewable electricity (%)",
                JoinBy = JoinSpec.Between("iso_a3", "code"),
                Data = SampleData.Renewables,
            },
        ],
    };
}</code></pre>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Getting started</h2>

<pre class="code-block"><code>dotnet add package Blazor-ApexMaps</code></pre>

<pre class="code-block"><code>// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddApexMaps(options =&gt;
{
    options.LicenseKey = "APEX-xxxxxxxx"; // optional: without one, licensed features are watermarked
});</code></pre>

<p>
    No script tag, no CDN reference and no stylesheet link: the package carries the apexmaps core as
    an ES module and the core injects its own CSS. Geometry is fetched only when a pack is named,
    one request per pack.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">The demos</h2>

<ul>
    <li><a href="choropleth">Choropleth</a> - the defaults, then normalising counts into rates.</li>
